 ## Background
-Rip-NeRF was introduced in the paper "Rip-NeRF: Anti-aliasing Radiance Fields with Ripmap-Encoded Platonic Solids" [S1]. It builds upon the hybrid representation approach of NeRF [Mildenhall et al. 2020] and area-sampling techniques from Mip-NeRF [Barron et al. 2021] and Tri-MipRF [Hu et al. 2023]. It specifically addresses the inefficiency of multi-sampling methods like Zip-NeRF [Barron et al. 2023] by introducing Ripmap-Encoded Platonic Solids for anisotropic area-sampling [S1].
+Rip-NeRF was introduced in the paper "Rip-NeRF: Anti-aliasing Radiance Fields with Ripmap-Encoded Platonic Solids" [S1]. It builds on the hybrid representation approach, combining the efficiency of explicit representations (3D Gaussians) with the flexibility of implicit representations (NeRF) [S1]. The method was developed to address the limitations of discrete point-based sampling and the inefficiency of multi-sampling mechanisms in existing methods like Zip-NeRF [S1].
 
